#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065375 :tutorial/content "To multiply a binomial and a trinomial, we take each term of the binomial and multiply it by every term in the trinomial. Then, we simplify by collecting like terms.\n\nFor example, to multiply $({\\color{blue}{x}} + {\\color{red}{3}})$ and $(x^2 + 2x+4)$, we multiply ${\\color{blue}{x}}$ and $(x^2 + 2x+4)$, and then we add this to the product of ${\\color{red}{3}}$ and $(x^2 + 2x+4)$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x + 3)(x^{2} + 2x + 4) &=  \\\\\nx(x^{2} + 2x + 4) + 3(x^{2} + 2x + 4) &=  \\\\\n&= x^{3} + 2x^{2} + 4x + 3x^{2} + 6x + 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we group the like terms and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{3} + 2x^{2} + 4x + 3x^{2} + 6x + 12 &=  \\\\\nx^{3} + 2x^{2} + 3x^{2} + 4x + 6x + 12 &=  \\\\\n&= x^{3} + 5x^{2} + 10x + 12\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "abdf5593-94a7-5f4c-a2ec-ca378d5b0034" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4089 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
